# Cloudflare Pages headers.
#
# The app is a static export that talks only to the cable. It makes NO network
# requests of its own at runtime, which is what lets the policy below be this
# tight: connect-src is 'self' purely so the page can fetch its own assets.
#
# If a feedback endpoint is added later (plan §8-1), connect-src gains exactly
# that origin and nothing else — widening it to https: would give away the one
# property that makes a diagnostic tool safe to leave open in a garage.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  # Both hardware permissions are stated, and both are stated as (self).
  #
  # `usb=()` is an EMPTY allowlist, not a default: it makes
  # navigator.usb.requestDevice() throw SecurityError on the deployed origin.
  # That is the Android path — Chrome for Android exposes a navigator.serial
  # that enumerates only Bluetooth RFCOMM, so a USB K+DCAN cable is unreachable
  # there and has to be driven over WebUSB. With `usb=()` every Android session
  # would fail in a way indistinguishable from an OTG or cable fault on the
  # phone, which is the worst place to spend an afternoon.
  #
  # `serial` is listed even though omitting it currently permits it. Once
  # someone is editing this line, an unstated permission is one deletion away
  # from breaking desktop, and the deletion would look deliberate.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(self), serial=(self)
  # noindex on EVERYTHING, not only on the data.
  #
  # It used to be `/*.json` and `/ecu-data/*`, because those are the files with
  # a provenance question attached (THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md §3). But the pages
  # that LINK them are just as findable, and this whole deployment is meant to
  # sit behind Cloudflare Access — a crawler reaching the index at all is a
  # symptom worth not having. It costs one header on assets nobody would index
  # anyway.
  #
  # This is a REQUEST, not access control. Access is what actually keeps the
  # tables private; `noindex` and `robots.txt` only ask politely.
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
